## 4. Linux-only calls in oodac/

### 4.1 Real shell invocations (must change for Windows)
- oda/oodac/cli_build.oo line 30: `readlink -f /proc/$PPID/exe` to find the host compiler. Windows: GetModuleFileName(NULL, …) or argv[0] resolution.
- oda/oodac/cli_build.oo line 46: same pattern, in the llvm backend.
- oda/oodac/cli_build.oo line 74: same pattern, for the host-stamp cache.
- oda/oodac/check_cache.oo line 87: `oo_file_stamp("/proc/self/exe")` to fingerprint the running compiler. Windows: GetModuleFileNameW.
- oda/oodac/main.oo line 178: `nm "$bin" | grep` to verify cap-require symbols in the output. Windows: dumpbin /SYMBOLS or objdump -t.
- oda/oodac/c_emit_sys.oo line 165: `process_exec` hardcodes `oo_str_lit("sh")` and `oo_str_lit("-c")`. Windows: `cmd /c` or `powershell -Command`. The runtime must choose.
- oda/oodac/cli_build.oo lines 32 and 34: `as --64` and `ld` (Linux binutils). Windows: clang/lld-link or mingw as/ld.
- oda/oodac/cli_build.oo line 48: `clang` from PATH. Windows: clang-cl or x86_64-w64-mingw32-clang.

### 4.2 String-literal names (no call site, just opaque identifiers the emitter prints)
- oda/oodac/c_emit_sys.oo lines 35 to 55: emits `oo_sys_epoll_create`, `oo_sys_inotify_init`, `oo_sys_prctl` as sealed stubs. Per c_emit_sys.oo line 5, these are "sealed stubs, not full async I/O." Windows: the stubs already return Err; the shim does not need to implement epoll/inotify/prctl semantics for the seed to build.
- oda/oodac/c_emit_call_ty.oo lines 23 to 24, tc_names_known.oo line 64, check_cap_util.oo lines 85 to 87: same names as strings in cap-classification tables.
- oda/oodac/c_emit_secret_alias.oo line 35, c_emit_secret_guard.oo lines 162 to 164 and 224: mmap, mprotect, shmat, pwrite, sendfile as data-sink name lists (no actual call).

### 4.3 None of the above is a C call
The compiler .oo source has no C-level Linux-only calls. The only blockers for a Windows build are the shell invocations in 4.1.

## 5. Build script for Windows

### 5.1 What oodac_pure_build does today
- Bash; lines 1 to 378.
- Discovers the host oodac binary (lines 10 to 17): SEED_OODAC, then oodac/oodac, then oodac_bin, then ooda/bootstrap/seed/oodac, then oodac/main.
- DFS-collects imports (lines 48 to 80); fail-closed on cycle or missing.
- Optional check gate (lines 102 to 143).
- Emits each module's C via $OODAC_BIN emit-c, with a sha256-keyed cache (lines 145 to 173).
- Python3 helper to dedupe function definitions and split bodies into ≤200-line chunks (lines 178 to 232).
- Appends runtime prototype block to preamble (lines 236 to 314).
- gcc command (line 349 to 350):
    gcc -O2 -I"$ROOT/runtime" ${OO_SSL_CFLAGS} runtime/chs_rt.c $TMP/all.c -lm -ldl -lpthread ${OO_SSL_LIBS} -o $OUT
- Self-test on outputs named oodac (lines 354 to 375): rejects if oodac-named output is the CLI, or fails to check fixtures/hello.oo.

### 5.2 What would have to change for mingw-w64
- Replace bash with PowerShell or cmd. The DFS and Python helper block is the bulk; both can be redone in PowerShell.
- Replace gcc with x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc (or clang --target=x86_64-windows-msvc / -windows-gnu).
- Add -D_WIN32_WINNT=0x0601 (Win 7) and -DWIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN. mingw provides windows.h, winsock2.h, ws2tcpip.h.
- Drop -ldl; replace with -lws2_32 (winsock), -lbcrypt (getrandom), -ladvapi32 (registry/limits), -liphlpapi (DNS). mingw maps pthread to winpthreads; on MSVC use the VStudio threading.
- Replace -lm with the mingw default (already linked).
- Skip -lssl/-lcrypto unless OO_HAVE_OPENSSL is satisfied; on Windows, OpenSSL is shipped as a separate DLL.
- Output: oodac.exe, a PE/COFF binary.
- The DFS-collect import graph is the same; the emit-c path is identical because the compiler source is pure .oo.
- The cap-require nm check (main.oo line 178) needs a Windows replacement: dumpbin /SYMBOLS or `llvm-readobj --coff-symbols`.

### 5.3 What would have to change for LLVM-windows (clang -target x86_64-pc-windows-msvc)
- The build is the same as mingw except the triple. clang handles winsock, bcrypt, advapi32 directly.
- The runtime shim (chs_rt_win32_*.c) is the dominant work; the build flags are minor.

## 6. Recommendation
1. The compiler source (ooda/oodac/*.oo) is portable. The Phase 1 cross-compile target (oodac --target windows) emits a Windows .exe from this Linux host; the .oo source needs no edits. Three shell invocations (cli_build.oo lines 30, 46, 74; check_cache.oo line 87; main.oo line 178; cli_build.oo line 165) need a `process_target` table that picks the right command per backend. The compiler owner owns this.
2. The C runtime is the bulk of the Windows work. chs_rt_sys.c, chs_rt_fs.c, chs_rt_thread.c, chs_rt_tls.c, chs_rt_dns.c, chs_rt_fetch.c, chs_rt_netfloor.c, chs_rt_ffi.c, chs_rt_ffi_sec.c, chs_rt_libfloor.c, chs_rt_meta.c, chs_rt_alloc.c, chs_rt_landlock.c, chs_rt_rlimit.c, chs_rt_time_rand.c, chs_rt_caps.c, chs_rt_actor.c, chs_rt_channel.c, chs_rt_arena.c, chs_rt_print.c, chs_rt_math.c each need a Windows shim. The runtime owner owns this.
4. Landlock and setrlimit (chs_rt_landlock.c, chs_rt_rlimit.c) have no direct Windows equivalent. They are stubs today (per c_emit_sys.oo:5 and chs_rt_landlock.c which uses prctl+syscall). The shim maps them to Job Objects + restricted tokens, per the master plan. Out of scope for sub-agent C; sub-agent B owns this.
4. The build script (ooda/bootstrap/oodac_pure_build) is bash and Linux-flavored. A mingw build needs a parallel PowerShell or cmd driver. The Phase 3 acceptance ("a Windows-native oodac.exe can check oodac/main.oo on a Windows host") needs both the compiler and the build driver. The build script is host-utility code; the compiler lane owns it but it is the gate for a real Windows rebuild.

## 7. What was not checked
- The seed binary ooda/bootstrap/seed/oodac: not present on this host. The .gitignore line 84 lists `/bootstrap/seed/oodac` as ignored; the directory contains only oodac.pub, oodac.sha256, oodac.sha256.minisig, README.oot, SIGNING.oot. file(1) and size were not directly observed.
- The Python3 dedup step in oodac_pure_build lines 178 to 232. Not executed; a Windows equivalent is required.
- Whether the existing emit-c path in ooda/oodac/ can be made target-aware without a new emit_windows.oo. The master plan names emit_windows.oo; this sub-agent did not read it.
- Whether mingw-w64 and llvm-mingw are installable on this Fedora host. Sub-agent A owns that question.
- The stragglers oda/runtime/chs_rt_str.c and oda/runtime/chs_rt_list_set.c: not exercised by the umbrella. Build behavior on Windows is unknown.
- chs_rt_stragglers' C contents (only the #include lines were read).
- oodac/.gitignore notes that oodac/oodac, oodac/oodac2, oodac/main are gitignored; a working tree rebuild is the everyday path, so the seed binary is irrelevant for the cross-compile lane.
